[Part B | Module 13: Permissions and Safety > Keeping Claude away from sensitive files / How to set it up in Claude Code]

Example: Asha's project folder contains a Payroll folder. She adds a deny rule for it. Later she asks Claude: "Summarize every file in this folder." Claude summarizes the other files, skips Payroll, and says it cannot open that folder.

In Claude Code, permission rules are kept in a settings file inside the project, named .claude/settings.json. Here is a small example that denies two things:

{

"permissions": {

"deny": ["Read(./.env)", "Read(./secrets/**)"]

}

}

Read(./.env) blocks the .env file in this project.

Read(./secrets/**) blocks every file inside the folder named secrets.

deny means Claude is never allowed to open them.

You do not have to write this by hand. Typing /permissions in Claude Code lets you view and change the rules. Because the file lives in the project, everyone who works in that project gets the same protection. Your company can also set rules that apply to everyone, so ask the AI Team for the standard settings.

Deny rules are one layer of protection, not the only one. Keep real passwords out of shared project folders where you can, and follow your company's security guidance.
